<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bobbio Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/614">614</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bobbio Abbey was a monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebbia</a> valley of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded in 614 by the Irish missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> on land granted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a>. It became one of the great centres of learning of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with a library of rare classical and Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. The abbey was suppressed under Napoleonic rule in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bobbio-abbey.jpg" alt="Bobbio Abbey" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Torsolo (uploader at en.wiki: Attilios) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_San_Colombano_-_Bobbio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The abbey was founded soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568. Under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> and of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>, a devout Roman Catholic, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and he granted Columbanus a ruined church and wasted lands known as Ebovium, which had belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> before the Lombards seized them. Columbanus wanted the secluded place because he preferred solitude for his monks. A monastery was built beside the little church of Saint Peter and at first followed the Rule of St Columbanus, based on the practices of Celtic Christianity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> was buried on 23 November 615. His successors Attala and Bertulf steered the house through the threats of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> under King Rotharis, and in 628 Bertulf persuaded Pope Honorius I to make the abbey subject directly to the Holy See. Under Abbot Bobolen the Rule of St. Benedict was introduced; it gradually replaced the stricter Columban rule. Bobbio became a centre of resistance to Arianism and a base for the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>.</p><p>In 774 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> made generous grants to the abbey, and late in the 9th century Abbot Agilulph moved the monastery farther downstream on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebbia</a>, where the medieval village grew up around it. In 1014 Emperor Henry II obtained from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-viii">Pope Benedict VIII</a> the creation of a bishopric of Bobbio, whose first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Peter Aldus, had been abbot since 999. The French occupying forces suppressed the abbey in 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A late 9th-century catalogue lists more than 600 volumes in the library, covering every branch of knowledge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Gerbert of Aurillac</a>, later Pope Sylvester II, became abbot in 982 and used its ancient treatises for his work on geometry. The books were later dispersed: in 1616 Cardinal Federico Borromeo took eighty-six volumes for the Ambrosian Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, among them the Antiphonary of Bangor, twenty-six went to Pope Paul V in 1618, and others to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, where many burned in the fire of 26 January 1904.</p><p>The present Basilica of San Colombano was built in 1456–1530 in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style; its crypt holds the sarcophagus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Columbanus</a> by Giovanni dei Patriarchi (1480) and a 12th-century mosaic pavement. The abbey's museum keeps the Bobbio collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a> ampullae, pilgrimage flasks of the 6th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bobbio Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Carolingians kept Lombard law and the palace at Pavia but installed Frankish counts and bishops, issued capitularies for Italy and supported monasteries such as <b>Bobbio</b> and Nonantola.</blockquote><a href="/state/carolingian-kingdom-of-italy">Carolingian Kingdom of Italy</a></li></ul></section>
